# awx-mcp — example environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and set YOUR OWN values. Every secret may be a literal OR a
# 1Password reference `op://<vault>/<item>/<field>`, resolved at runtime via
# the mcp-common credential chain (`op` + op-forward Touch ID, keyctl cache).
# This file commits only VARIABLE NAMES and example `op://` REFERENCES — never
# real secret values. The `op://` strings below are pointers, not secrets.
#
# Per-user / non-shared: each person owns their own 1Password items (personal
# `Employee` vault) and points these vars at their own `op://` refs. The
# `op://` field label depends on the item category:
#   - LOGIN item          → /username, /password
#   - API_CREDENTIAL item → /credential
#
# 1Password tagging convention (tag YOUR personal items — see
# ../../docs/AGENT_CONVENTIONS.md#per-user-credentials-host-url-defaults-and-1password-tagging):
#   - common tag:      awx-mcp
#   - per-service tag: awx
#   - TAG, don't rename, SHARED items; REUSE one item across servers for the
#     same secret.
#
# Validate setup:  uv run mcp-plugin-gen doctor .   &&   uv run mcp-common-doctor
# op-forward gotcha: `op` colorizes even `--format json`; set NO_COLOR=1 when
# scripting around it or the JSON won't parse.

# ── AWX API auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AWX OAuth2 Personal Access Token — API_CREDENTIAL item → /credential.
# Example item: "AWX" in the Employee vault (item id
# 4aq4piohzi3jmmppus36gctyxa), tagged `awx-mcp`. Alias: CONTROLLER_OAUTH_TOKEN.
AWX_TOKEN="op://Employee/AWX/credential"

# ── Host URL: non-secret, default-but-overridable ────────────────────────
# Shows the BUILT-IN DEFAULT. Leave unset to use it; the common case needs no
# AWX_HOST. Override with a literal, or (same resolver) an `op://` ref — the
# AWX item also exposes a `hostname` field, e.g. "op://Employee/AWX/hostname".
# Alias: CONTROLLER_HOST.
# AWX_HOST="https://awx.internal.together.ai/"

# ── HTTP transport auth (only when running `--transport http`) ───────────
# Separate access token guarding the MCP tool surface (distinct from AWX_TOKEN).
# Required for HTTP transport; unused by the default stdio transport. May be a
# literal or an `op://` ref. Alias: AWX_MCP_HTTP_ACCESS_TOKEN.
# MCP_HTTP_ACCESS_TOKEN="op://Employee/awx-mcp HTTP access/credential"
